\section{Which mark becomes which}{
Marks tween by \strong{identity}, as gganimate matches by group: each mark of one
state moves into the mark that \emph{is the same thing} in the next state, however
the rows are ordered and whatever groups each state contains. A mark's
identity is
\enumerate{
\item its \code{data_id}, when the layer declares one (\code{mark_point(data_id = country)}): the way to follow individual observations;
\item otherwise its group -- the \code{group} aesthetic if mapped, else the
combination of the layer's discrete mapped aesthetics (\code{color}, \code{fill},
\code{shape}, ..., and a discrete \code{x}, \code{y} or \code{label}) -- so a bar race's bars,
filled by country, slide between ranks keeping their colours;
\item and within a group, the order of its rows -- the last resort, enough when
each group has one mark per state, but rows of one group pair up by
position. Give such a layer a \code{data_id} (or a \code{group}) when its rows are
individual observations whose order may change.
}

A line, path or area is one mark per series and is matched by its group.

A faceted plot animates panel by panel: every panel of the whole data is
drawn in every frame (a state without rows for a panel leaves it empty
rather than dropping it), and a mark's identity includes its panel, so a
mark never slides from one panel into another -- a row whose facet value
changes between states fades out of one panel and into the other. The
scales are frozen across frames, so per-panel scales (\code{facet_wrap(scales = "free")}, an independent \code{resolve_scale()}) are refused.

A mark found in only one of two adjacent states fades in or out (points,
bars and tiles, labels, lines and areas alike, with their effect underlays
such as \code{\link[=glow]{glow()}}) on the alpha easing, and a point whose colour group changes
slides to its new place while its colour blends. A line, path or area whose
number of points changes morphs from one outline into the other. Where marks
overlap, the stacking order switches to the next state's halfway through the
transition. What cannot move switches whole halfway through: decoration
without an identity that comes and goes, or a line that cannot be morphed (a
broken line with missing values, mixed units). \code{animate()} warns (once per
call) when that will happen, naming the states.

Give each transition enough frames to be seen: \code{nframes} is spread over all
the states, so 40 states in 80 frames leave two frames per state and nothing
visibly moves.
}

\section{Frame variables}{
A title, subtitle or caption (set with \code{\link[=labs]{labs()}}) may embed frame variables
in braces, glue-style, as in gganimate: \code{labs(title = "Year: {frame_time}")}.
Each \code{{...}} is an R expression evaluated per frame with the variables below
in scope (and the environment \code{animate()} was called from behind them), so
\code{"{round(frame_time)}"} or \code{"{format(frame_time, '\%Y')}"} work; write \verb{\{\{}
and \verb{\}\}} for literal braces. The label is updated on \strong{every frame}, not
only at keyframes: a \code{{frame_time}} title counts through the in-between
times while the marks move.
\itemize{
\item every transition: \code{frame} (the frame number), \code{nframes}, \code{progress}
(\code{frame / nframes});
\item \code{\link[=transition_states]{transition_states()}}: \code{closest_state}, \code{previous_state}, \code{next_state}
(the state the frame is nearest to, comes from and goes to; all three are
the held state during a pause) and \code{transitioning} (\code{TRUE} while moving).
\code{closest_state} switches halfway through a transition, on the position
easing, when the marks' discrete attributes do;
\item \code{\link[=transition_time]{transition_time()}}: \code{frame_time}, the time the frame has reached (on the
position easing, so it agrees with where the marks are), of the time
column's class (\code{Date}, \code{POSIXct}, or double);
\item \code{\link[=transition_reveal]{transition_reveal()}}: \code{frame_along}, the \code{along} value revealed so far.
}

A \code{\link[=mark_text]{mark_text()}} label can use the same variables (VP-93): \code{mark_text(data = data.frame(x = 3000, y = 40), x = x, y = y, label = "{round(frame_time)}", size = 60)} draws the year large behind a gapminder bubble chart, counting
on every frame. A constant label or a column of labels may contain \code{{...}};
the label is placed (and moves) as the mark it belongs to, and only its
string changes per frame. Repelled and rich-text labels are not templated.
An ordinary data label whose string differs between two states takes the
nearer state's string, switching halfway through.
}

\section{Pauses and rewind}{
\code{start_pause} and \code{end_pause} hold the first and last frame for that many
frames, and \code{rewind = TRUE} plays the animation back to the start, as in
gganimate. They come out of \code{nframes}: \code{animate(nframes = 100, end_pause = 20)} moves for 80 frames and rests on the final picture for 20. Every output
honours them -- \code{\link[=anim_save]{anim_save()}} to GIF, APNG or SVG, \code{\link[=anim_frame]{anim_frame()}}'s frame
numbers, and the frame scrubber of an animated \code{vellumwidget}.
}

